771484375, 0.031951904296875, -0.039764404296875, 0.00904083251953125, 0.0262298583984375, 0.0272369384765625, 0.046966552734375, -0.006839752197265625, -0.01275634765625, 0.005260467529296875, 0.0239410400390625, 0.01580810546875, -0.00247955322265625, -0.0007472038269042969, 0.01076507568359375, 0.031494140625, -0.0284423828125, 0.049346923828125, -0.0197906494140625, 0.019317626953125, -0.0042266845703125, -0.036346435546875, 0.04669189453125, 0.03057861328125, 0.00994110107421875, 0.003284454345703125, 0.016143798828125, 0.00988006591796875, 0.040313720703125, -0.005382537841796875, -0.0230255126953125, 0.015655517578125, -0.01421356201171875, 0.0003190040588378906, -0.0307159423828125, -0.0302276611328125, 0.0228729248046875, 0.03021240234375, -0.01056671142578125, 0.005390167236328125, -0.016143798828125, -0.0136260986328125, -0.02532958984375, -0.015869140625, 0.017547607421875, 0.0012254714965820312, -0.0255584716796875, 0.0499267578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "An antibiotic is a type of antimicrobial substance that is specifically active against bacteria. Antimicrobials are a broader category of agents that act against various types of microorganisms, including bacteria, viruses, fungi, and protozoa. While all antibiotics are antimicrobials, not all antimicrobials are antibiotics; for example, antiviral and antifungal drugs target viruses and fungi, respectively, rather than bacteria.\n\n**Mechanisms of Action**\n\nAntibiotics are primarily used in clinical and veterinary settings to treat and prevent bacterial infections in humans and animals. They function through two main mechanisms: bactericidal antibiotics kill bacteria directly, while bacteriostatic antibiotics inhibit bacterial growth and reproduction, allowing the immune system to eliminate the pathogens. For example, penicillin is bactericidal, whereas tetracycline is bacteriostatic.\n\n**Exceptions and Limitations**\n\nAlthough antibiotics are designed to target bacteria, some, such as metronidazole, also exhibit activity against certain protozoa. This exception is significant because it highlights that the classification of antibiotics is based mainly on their primary targets, but some agents may have broader effects.\n\nAntibiotics are not effective against viral or most fungal infections. This is because viruses lack the cellular structures, such as cell walls or ribosomes, that antibiotics typically target, and fungi have different cell wall compositions and metabolic pathways compared to bacteria.\n\n**Antibiotic Resistance**\n\nA critical issue associated with antibiotic use is the development of antibiotic resistance. Overuse or misuse of antibiotics in medicine and agriculture can lead to the emergence of resistant bacterial strains, making infections harder to treat and posing a significant public health challenge."}}